# Citation metadata for the repository (Citation File Format 1.2.0).
# Draft of 2026-10-23. Placeholders to fill before publication: affiliation,
# ORCID, repository URL, release date. Validate with cffconvert or the
# GitHub citation widget after the first push.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use the method, the factor tables or the package, please cite this repository and the source reviews listed in the factor tables."
title: "wash-health-impact-tool: estimating health outcomes from verified water and sanitation outputs for outcome-based finance"
type: software
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-10-23"  # placeholder: set to the public release date
license:
  - MIT
  - CC-BY-4.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/ORGANISATION/wash-health-impact-tool"  # placeholder
authors:
  - family-names: "Schöbitz"
    given-names: "Lars"
    affiliation: "AFFILIATION"  # placeholder: pick one of the two affiliations, or none
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"  # placeholder
keywords:
  - water
  - sanitation
  - hygiene
  - health impact estimation
  - diarrhoea
  - outcome-based finance
  - SIINC
  - R package
abstract: >-
  An evidence review, a set of versioned conversion factor tables, a
  methodology, additionality guidance and an R package (washhealth) that turn
  verified water, sanitation and hygiene outputs of enterprises into estimated
  health outcomes (diarrhoea cases, deaths and DALYs averted, people reached)
  with ranges, a confidence label and a ledger of every published number used.
  Built on 2026-10-23 as a scratch reproduction of the Aqua for All assignment
  "Developing a water and sanitation - health impact estimation tool for
  outcome-based finance". The search, screening, extraction, code, tests and
  documents were produced by an AI agent (Claude, Anthropic) working under
  Lars Schöbitz's decisions and instructions; the human review of the factor
  rows and of the screening decisions is recorded in data/factors/CHANGELOG.md.
  Code is MIT licensed; factor tables and documents are CC BY 4.0.
references:
  - type: article
    title: "Effectiveness of interventions to improve drinking water, sanitation, and handwashing with soap on risk of diarrhoeal disease in children in low-income and middle-income settings: a systematic review and meta-analysis"
    authors:
      - family-names: "Wolf"
        given-names: "Jennyfer"
      - name: "and colleagues"
    journal: "The Lancet"
    volume: 400
    year: 2022
    doi: "10.1016/s0140-6736(22)00937-0"
  - type: report
    title: "Burden of disease attributable to unsafe drinking-water, sanitation and hygiene: 2019 update"
    authors:
      - name: "World Health Organization"
    year: 2023
    url: "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240075610"
